    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d388">

                <pb facs="0335" n="329" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg329"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1115/4317</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2219</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-05-22">May 22, 1939</date>.<lb/>[Received June
                        28.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to submit with this
                    despatch a copy of a self-explanatory communication that the Acting Consul
                    General for Japan in Shanghai addressed to me on May 19, 1939,<anchor n="72"
                        xml:id="d388-f1"/> offering on behalf of the Japanese military authorities
                    the limited facilities of Japanese transports to Americans who evacuated Kuling
                    prior to the Japanese military operations against that place and who now desire
                    to return there.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
